SPECIFICATION: Host Plugin Interface for Audio Effects
Version: 1.0
Goal: A drop-in Python plugin module discoverable by host.py. Another implementer should be able to wrap ANY effect (including reverb) if they obey this interface—without needing the reverb DSP spec.

==============================================================================
1. DISCOVERY
==============================================================================
The host imports Python modules and treats a module as a plugin if it defines a class that exposes ALL of:
  get_name(self) -> str
  get_knobs(self) -> List[Knob]
  get_switches(self) -> List[Switch]
  apply_effect(self, audio, sample_rate: int)

Optional but recommended:
  get_colors(self) -> dict[str, str]

The plugin class is instantiated once (or once per chain slot). The instance is long-lived. The host mutates instance attributes, then calls apply_effect.

==============================================================================
2. SHARED DATACLASSES (must exist in the plugin module OR be imported from the host’s identical definitions)
==============================================================================
from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from typing import List

@dataclass
class KnobColorScheme:
    outline: str = "black"
    face: str = "white"
    needle: str = "black"

@dataclass
class Knob:
    name: str = "Generic"
    min: float = 0.0
    max: float = 1.0
    default: float = 0.5
    click_pts: List[float] = field(default_factory=lambda: [0.5])
    knob_color_scheme: KnobColorScheme = field(
        default_factory=lambda: KnobColorScheme(outline="black", face="white", needle="black")
    )

@dataclass
class Switch:
    name: str = "Enable"
    default: bool = True

Rules:
  - Knob.name and Switch.name are UI labels AND the source of the live attribute name (see §4).
  - min < max. default must lie in [min, max].
  - click_pts are suggested detent values; they do not change the continuous range unless the host snaps.
  - Colors are CSS hex or CSS color names.

==============================================================================
3. REQUIRED METHODS
==============================================================================
get_name()
  Human-readable plugin title for the rack/GUI. Example: "Reverb".

get_knobs()
  Returns the full ordered list of knobs. Order is display order.
  Called at UI build time; do not change the list later unless the host rebuilds.

get_switches()
  Returns the full ordered list of boolean switches. Order is display order.
  Convention: first switch is often Enable.

get_colors()  [optional]
  Returns a dict used by the host skin. Expected keys (all hex strings):
    face, edge, text, muted,
    button, button_hot, button_on, button_text,
    switch_on, switch_off,
    slider, slider_handle,
    knob_outline, knob_face, knob_needle
  Missing keys may fall back to host defaults.

apply_effect(audio, sample_rate: int)
  audio: NumPy array float-like in [-1, 1], shape (n,) or (n, channels)
  sample_rate: int Hz
  MUST return a NumPy array:
    - same length n
    - same number of dimensions and channel count as input
    - finite values
  MUST be safe for audio == empty.
  MUST NOT assume extra keyword arguments; all parameters come from instance attributes.
  MUST honor Enable-style bypass: if the enable attribute is False, return audio unchanged.

==============================================================================
4. LIVE PARAMETER BINDING (critical)
==============================================================================
The host does NOT call apply_effect(gain=...). It writes fields onto the instance first.

Binding rule:
  attribute_name = knob_or_switch.name.strip().lower()
  with spaces removed or replaced consistently. THIS REVERB PLUGIN uses these exact attribute names (already lowercase, no spaces):

    Knobs:
      Mix         -> self.mix
      Decay       -> self.decay
      Predelay    -> self.predelay
      Brightness  -> self.brightness
      Damp        -> self.damp
      Style       -> self.style

    Switches:
      Enable      -> self.enable
      Tail        -> self.tail

Implementers MUST initialize these attributes in __init__ to the same defaults advertised by get_knobs/get_switches so apply_effect works before the GUI moves anything.

Recommended __init__ defaults for this plugin:
  self.name = "Reverb"
  self.enable = True
  self.mix = 0.32
  self.decay = 2.0
  self.predelay = 18.0
  self.brightness = 0.6
  self.damp = 0.35
  self.style = 1.0
  self.tail = False

Inside apply_effect, read with getattr(self, "mix", default) so a partial host cannot crash the plugin. Clip to the knob min/max.

Style is a float knob 0..7 with click_pts [0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7]. The plugin must round to nearest int and clip to 0..7.

==============================================================================
5. KNOB / SWITCH DEFINITIONS FOR THIS PLUGIN (interface only)
==============================================================================
Knob "Mix"        min=0 max=1     default=0.32  click_pts=[0.0, 0.15, 0.32, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0]
Knob "Decay"      min=0.2 max=8   default=2.0   click_pts=[0.4, 0.8, 1.2, 2.0, 3.5, 6.0, 8.0]
Knob "Predelay"   min=0 max=120   default=18    click_pts=[0.0, 12.0, 18.0, 30.0, 60.0, 90.0]
Knob "Brightness" min=0 max=1     default=0.6   click_pts=[0.0, 0.25, 0.45, 0.6, 0.8, 1.0]
Knob "Damp"       min=0 max=0.95  default=0.35  click_pts=[0.0, 0.2, 0.35, 0.55, 0.75, 0.95]
Knob "Style"      min=0 max=7     default=1.0   click_pts=list(range(8))

Switch "Enable" default True
Switch "Tail"   default False

Suggested KnobColorScheme for all knobs:
  outline="#0e1214" face="#6a8a92" needle="#f2f7f8"

Suggested get_colors palette:
  face="#1c2428" edge="#0e1214" text="#e8f0f2" muted="#8aa0a8"
  button="#2c3a40" button_hot="#3d525a" button_on="#2a7a6a" button_text="#e8f0f2"
  switch_on="#2a7a6a" switch_off="#1a2226"
  slider="#141c20" slider_handle="#8aa0a8"
  knob_outline="#0e1214" knob_face="#6a8a92" knob_needle="#f2f7f8"

==============================================================================
6. APPLY_EFFECT I/O RULES (host compatibility, not DSP)
==============================================================================
- Do not change sample_rate.
- Do not return a longer or shorter buffer than len(audio) along axis 0.
- Preserve channel count.
- Input may be float32 or float64; processing may promote internally but returning float32 is acceptable if input was float32. Do not return int PCM.
- No file I/O, no prints, no GUI calls inside apply_effect.
- Must be re-entrant per instance in the sense that consecutive calls with new audio use the current attribute values. Internal caches (e.g. last IR) are allowed if keyed by parameters + sample_rate.
- Threading: assume the host calls apply_effect from one audio/UI thread at a time unless documented otherwise.

==============================================================================
7. CLASS SHAPE CHECKLIST
==============================================================================
class Reverb:
    def __init__(self): ... set name + live params ...
    def get_name(self) -> str: ...
    def get_colors(self): ...
    def get_knobs(self) -> List[Knob]: ...
    def get_switches(self) -> List[Switch]: ...
    def apply_effect(self, audio, sample_rate: int): ...

Reference pattern (Volume Boost):
  live attrs self.gain, self.enable
  get_knobs returns Knob(name="Gain", ...)
  get_switches returns Switch(name="Enable", ...)
  apply_effect reads getattr(self, "enable") and getattr(self, "gain")

A second LLM implementing ONLY this interface spec should produce a class the host can load. The DSP body of apply_effect can be a stub (return audio * mix-shaped gain) until the effect spec is applied.